VERIFICATION
  Isolation check green, ao-build-update asserted Internal=false at 10.89.13.0/24.
  Unit is generated and is "generated", not enabled: it carries no WantedBy=.
  Host-side regression, all six cases as expected:
    allowlisted + digest-pinned        -> 0
    unpinned tag                       -> 4
    not-allowlisted host               -> 4
    denied host (packages.ros.org)     -> 4
    unqualified reference              -> 2
    ghcr.io + digest-pinned            -> 0
  Container-side: unpinned tag -> 4, denied host -> 4, default --plan -> 0.
  The container run wrote through its bind mounts to the host evidence paths
  /ALWAYSON/logs/operations/build-update-audit.log and
  /ALWAYSON/data/build-update/staging/, confirmed on the host.
  --fetch against a digest absent from the local store reports resolved=false
  and resolved_digest=null. It does not claim a resolution it did not make.

THREE DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED DURING VERIFICATION
  1. ao-build-update.network was missing its [Network] group header, so
     quadlet-generator rejected it ("key file does not start with a group") and
     no service was generated. Caught only by reading the generator log, not by
     the deploy script, which reported success. Header restored.
  2. The allowlist listed registry-1.docker.io, the Docker Hub API endpoint,
     while image references carry the docker.io prefix. Every legitimate
     docker.io reference was being rejected as NOT-ALLOWLISTED. Corrected to
     docker.io with the endpoint recorded as a comment, because the two are not
     the same string.
  3. The acquisition script hardcoded /ALWAYSON paths, which do not exist
     inside the container. First real unit run exited 3 (config error). The
     script now resolves its allowlist, staging dir, and audit log through
     AO_ROOT and three explicit environment overrides set by the unit, so a host
     run and a container run write evidence to the same files.

OPEN, FOR THE OPERATOR
  - Enabling the unit permanently, and any decision to automate acquisition.
    It reaches the public internet, so this is an operator decision.
  - The build/update scope question: the name implies builds, the specified role
    is updates only. The Gazebo Containerfiles under GAZEBO/containers/ have no
    owner, no digest capture, and no update audit. Recorded in README 5.2.1.
  - packages.ros.org stays on the adapter deny list. It fails TLS verification
    from this host (cert CN=*.osuosl.org does not cover the name) and that
    verification is deliberately not disabled.
